00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,922
Welcome to BIOL-1 General Biology at College of the Redwoods, Pelican Bay.

2
00:00:05,922 --> 00:00:08,459
Module 13: How Populations Evolve.

3
00:00:08,459 --> 00:00:12,265
The connected lab is Lab 13 How Populations Evolve.

4
00:00:12,265 --> 00:00:18,310
Here is the central idea: How Populations Evolve explains allele-frequency change

5
00:00:18,310 --> 00:00:23,608
through drift, gene flow, selection, and baseline models. Let us begin.

6
00:00:24,358 --> 00:00:29,006
By the end of this module you should be able to: Define microevolution using allele

7
00:00:29,006 --> 00:00:33,710
frequencies; Compare the five mechanisms that change allele frequencies; Distinguish

8
00:00:33,710 --> 00:00:38,302
bottleneck and founder effects; Interpret directional, stabilizing, and disruptive

9
00:00:38,302 --> 00:00:41,494
selection; Use Hardy-Weinberg as a non-evolving baseline.

10
00:00:41,494 --> 00:00:45,022
Keep these objectives in mind as you work through the material.

11
00:00:45,772 --> 00:00:47,616
This module covers 5 topics.

12
00:00:47,616 --> 00:00:52,687
Allele frequencies as the unit of change — Microevolution is change in allele

13
00:00:52,687 --> 00:00:54,729
frequencies across generations.

14
00:00:54,729 --> 00:00:59,603
Sources of variation — Mutation creates new variation, while recombination

15
00:00:59,603 --> 00:01:01,579
reshuffles existing variation.

16
00:01:01,579 --> 00:01:06,914
Five mechanisms of microevolution — Mutation, gene flow, genetic drift, nonrandom

17
00:01:06,914 --> 00:01:10,405
mating, and natural selection can change populations.

18
00:01:10,405 --> 00:01:15,410
Genetic drift and selection — Genetic drift is random and strongest in small

19
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:19,098
populations; selection is nonrandom and tied to fitness.

20
00:01:19,098 --> 00:01:24,169
Hardy-Weinberg as a baseline — Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium is a null model for

21
00:01:24,169 --> 00:01:26,145
detecting evolutionary change.

22
00:01:26,145 --> 00:01:30,820
Each topic builds on the one before it, forming a coherent explanation.

23
00:01:31,570 --> 00:01:34,081
Here is the concept map for module 13.

24
00:01:34,081 --> 00:01:39,170
The central claim is: How Populations Evolve explains allele-frequency change

25
00:01:39,170 --> 00:01:42,937
through drift, gene flow, selection, and baseline models.

26
00:01:42,937 --> 00:01:45,845
Notice how each node connects to the others.

27
00:01:45,845 --> 00:01:51,397
This is the structure of biological reasoning — not a list to memorize, but a map of

28
00:01:51,397 --> 00:01:53,314
